1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,920
 At this point, I've had lots of practice telling you

2
00:00:01,920 --> 00:00:03,400
 that this isn't a chemistry course,

3
00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:05,640
 but we will need to talk about the chemistry

4
00:00:05,640 --> 00:00:07,560
 and the physics of engineering polymers

5
00:00:07,560 --> 00:00:10,480
 to understand why it is that they have the properties

6
00:00:10,480 --> 00:00:11,640
 that they have.

7
00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:13,520
 In this video, we're only going to scratch the surface

8
00:00:13,520 --> 00:00:15,520
 of this physics and chemistry background,

9
00:00:15,520 --> 00:00:18,520
 but I will link you to some other more in-depth resources

10
00:00:18,520 --> 00:00:20,080
 on the textbook page for this video

11
00:00:20,080 --> 00:00:21,800
 if you're interested in checking those out.

12
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:23,360
 To begin with, I think we'll define

13
00:00:23,360 --> 00:00:25,840
 that polymers really are just long chains

14
00:00:25,840 --> 00:00:28,400
 of repeating chemical molecules, units,

15
00:00:28,400 --> 00:00:30,440
 that we call monomers.

16
00:00:30,440 --> 00:00:31,760
 If you zoom in from a bulk polymer,

17
00:00:31,760 --> 00:00:32,760
 you'll see these chains,

18
00:00:32,760 --> 00:00:34,720
 and if you zoom in again onto one chain,

19
00:00:34,720 --> 00:00:35,600
 you'll see that really,

20
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:37,840
 it's just made up of these repeating units

21
00:00:37,840 --> 00:00:39,520
 over and over and over again.

22
00:00:39,520 --> 00:00:41,440
 The bulk properties of an engineering polymer

23
00:00:41,440 --> 00:00:43,760
 are largely a function of what these chains are made of

24
00:00:43,760 --> 00:00:45,960
 and how the chains themselves are held together

25
00:00:45,960 --> 00:00:47,840
 and how the different chains of a polymer

26
00:00:47,840 --> 00:00:50,040
 interact with each other within the bulk.

27
00:00:50,040 --> 00:00:52,440
 Unlike metals, which were held together with metallic bonds,

28
00:00:52,440 --> 00:00:54,240
 the electron sharing arrangement

29
00:00:54,240 --> 00:00:56,280
 that gave them a lot of their nice thermal

30
00:00:56,280 --> 00:00:57,640
 and electrical properties.

31
00:00:57,680 --> 00:01:00,600
 Polymers are held together with covalent bonds,

32
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:02,560
 very, very strong bonds that arise

33
00:01:02,560 --> 00:01:04,600
 from an electron sharing arrangement.

34
00:01:04,600 --> 00:01:06,720
 Without delving too deeply into the chemistry,

35
00:01:06,720 --> 00:01:09,360
 I'll say that covalent bonds are generally motivated

36
00:01:09,360 --> 00:01:12,840
 by the desire of an atom to fill its outermost electron shell,

37
00:01:12,840 --> 00:01:14,640
 its valence electron shell.

38
00:01:14,640 --> 00:01:16,680
 If a particular atom is somewhat short

39
00:01:16,680 --> 00:01:18,120
 of filling this valence shell,

40
00:01:18,120 --> 00:01:19,880
 then it's going to go looking for other atoms

41
00:01:19,880 --> 00:01:23,440
 that it can share electrons with covalently bond with

42
00:01:23,440 --> 00:01:25,440
 so that hopefully they can both come closer

43
00:01:25,440 --> 00:01:27,080
 to having a full shell.

44
00:01:27,080 --> 00:01:29,360
 Most engineering polymers are what we call organic,

45
00:01:29,360 --> 00:01:31,880
 meaning that they're largely composed of carbon.

46
00:01:31,880 --> 00:01:34,000
 Carbon is exactly for electron short

47
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:35,240
 of filling its outer shell.

48
00:01:35,240 --> 00:01:38,360
 And consequently, it would like to make four covalent bonds

49
00:01:38,360 --> 00:01:40,240
 if it could to do so.

50
00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:42,760
 In organic polymers, this manifests as carbon atoms

51
00:01:42,760 --> 00:01:44,680
 forming the backbone of a long chain

52
00:01:44,680 --> 00:01:48,840
 and small fingers coming off each carbon atom to other atoms.

53
00:01:48,840 --> 00:01:50,680
 Here, each carbon atom is quite happy.

54
00:01:50,680 --> 00:01:52,360
 It's made one bond to each of its neighbors

55
00:01:52,360 --> 00:01:54,040
 and one bond to each of the two fingers

56
00:01:54,040 --> 00:01:55,080
 that are attached to it as well.

57
00:01:55,480 --> 00:01:57,320
 Consequently, by making four bonds,

58
00:01:57,320 --> 00:01:59,760
 it's completely filled up its valence shell.

59
00:01:59,760 --> 00:02:02,760
 A bulk polymer consists of many, many of these chains,

60
00:02:02,760 --> 00:02:04,960
 each of which are very, very long.

61
00:02:04,960 --> 00:02:07,240
 Each of these polymer chains can consist of hundreds

62
00:02:07,240 --> 00:02:10,240
 of repetitions of this kind of smallest base unit

63
00:02:10,240 --> 00:02:12,360
 all the way up to thousands or tens of thousands

64
00:02:12,360 --> 00:02:13,600
 of repetitions.

65
00:02:13,600 --> 00:02:16,080
 And in the bulk, the way that these chains are arranged,

66
00:02:16,080 --> 00:02:18,120
 the way that they're ordered has a pretty substantial

67
00:02:18,120 --> 00:02:19,600
 effect on their properties.

68
00:02:19,600 --> 00:02:22,360
 Occasionally, polymer chains can form in a reasonably

69
00:02:22,360 --> 00:02:24,400
 ordered arrangement that we call crystalline,

70
00:02:24,400 --> 00:02:27,080
 a little bit like the metals that we met two weeks ago.

71
00:02:27,080 --> 00:02:29,600
 But most of the time, polymer chains are quite amorphous,

72
00:02:29,600 --> 00:02:32,320
 quite unordered and messy, all over the place,

73
00:02:32,320 --> 00:02:35,560
 kind of entangled and intertwined and interlinked.

74
00:02:35,560 --> 00:02:37,920
 Crystalline or not, there are two main ways

75
00:02:37,920 --> 00:02:39,720
 that polymer chains connect to each other

76
00:02:39,720 --> 00:02:41,960
 to make up a bulk polymer.

77
00:02:41,960 --> 00:02:43,640
 The first way called cross-linking

78
00:02:43,640 --> 00:02:46,880
 is where one polymer chain branches off into another one.

79
00:02:46,880 --> 00:02:48,840
 Essentially, from one finger of chain one

80
00:02:48,840 --> 00:02:51,880
 begins a linking chain that connects it to chain two.

81
00:02:51,880 --> 00:02:53,680
 This is quite a robust method of attachment

82
00:02:53,760 --> 00:02:54,720
 of two polymer chains,

83
00:02:54,720 --> 00:02:56,960
 because essentially it consists of covalent bonds,

84
00:02:56,960 --> 00:02:59,080
 which are even stronger than the metallic one

85
00:02:59,080 --> 00:03:00,640
 that we met two weeks ago.

86
00:03:00,640 --> 00:03:02,760
 But for most polymers, cross-linking like this

87
00:03:02,760 --> 00:03:05,600
 isn't the dominant method of attachment between chains.

88
00:03:05,600 --> 00:03:08,480
 Instead, most bulk polymers consist of polymer chains

89
00:03:08,480 --> 00:03:10,800
 that are held together electrostatically

90
00:03:10,800 --> 00:03:13,840
 through reasonably weak, Vanderwalls bonds.

91
00:03:13,840 --> 00:03:16,160
 I won't get into too much detail about Vanderwalls bonds.

92
00:03:16,160 --> 00:03:17,920
 I'll leave a link for you on the textbook page

93
00:03:17,920 --> 00:03:20,040
 because it's a little bit complicated how they work.

94
00:03:20,040 --> 00:03:22,120
 But if you're going to understand one thing about them,

95
00:03:22,120 --> 00:03:23,960
 you should understand that they're reasonably weak,

96
00:03:23,960 --> 00:03:26,680
 essentially only providing a little bit of stickiness

97
00:03:26,680 --> 00:03:28,880
 between the chains as opposed to covalent bonds,

98
00:03:28,880 --> 00:03:32,240
 which provide a really, really strong robust link.

99
00:03:32,240 --> 00:03:33,880
 The mechanical properties of a polymer

100
00:03:33,880 --> 00:03:36,840
 are largely a function of which of these types of bonds

101
00:03:36,840 --> 00:03:39,640
 and in what proportion these types of bonds are present

102
00:03:39,640 --> 00:03:41,360
 to hold the chains together.

103
00:03:41,360 --> 00:03:44,440
 Most into chain bonds in polymers are Vanderwalls bonds

104
00:03:44,440 --> 00:03:46,320
 that give way pretty easily.

105
00:03:46,320 --> 00:03:47,960
 This is what gives a lot of these polymers

106
00:03:47,960 --> 00:03:50,480
 their low strength and relative ductility.

107
00:03:50,480 --> 00:03:53,000
 In this way, polymers held together by Vanderwalls bonds

108
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:54,960
 are a little bit like wet spaghetti.

109
00:03:54,960 --> 00:03:56,720
 Each strand is itself quite strong

110
00:03:56,720 --> 00:03:58,840
 if you can load just that strand up

111
00:03:58,840 --> 00:04:01,640
 because it's held together by nice strong covalent bonds.

112
00:04:01,640 --> 00:04:04,360
 But if you kind of pull on the entire assembly

113
00:04:04,360 --> 00:04:07,000
 of wet spaghetti, rather than transferring the load

114
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:09,200
 directly through the backbones of the chains

115
00:04:09,200 --> 00:04:10,880
 through the strands of spaghetti,

116
00:04:10,880 --> 00:04:13,880
 instead they have a tendency to just slide over each other,

117
00:04:13,880 --> 00:04:15,240
 slip past each other.

118
00:04:15,240 --> 00:04:18,880
 The inter-strand stiffness is just not particularly robust

119
00:04:18,960 --> 00:04:21,160
 compared to the strength of the backbones

120
00:04:21,160 --> 00:04:22,960
 of the polymer chains.

121
00:04:22,960 --> 00:04:24,520
 While humans have been working with polymers

122
00:04:24,520 --> 00:04:26,880
 in one form or another for thousands of years,

123
00:04:26,880 --> 00:04:29,520
 we only really started to mass produce these things

124
00:04:29,520 --> 00:04:31,840
 to really get going in the last 80 years

125
00:04:31,840 --> 00:04:33,640
 around the time of the Second World War.

126
00:04:33,640 --> 00:04:35,800
 When restrictions and shortages on natural fibers

127
00:04:35,800 --> 00:04:37,400
 provide a sufficient motivation for us

128
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:39,000
 to begin to look into these things.

129
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:41,440
 What's kind of incredible is that over the last 80 years,

130
00:04:41,440 --> 00:04:43,200
 we've near-perfected this process.

131
00:04:43,200 --> 00:04:45,200
 We've gotten almost as good at making polymers

132
00:04:45,200 --> 00:04:46,800
 as we have it making steel.

133
00:04:46,800 --> 00:04:48,400
 If you look around you, you'd be hard-pressed not

134
00:04:48,400 --> 00:04:50,200
 to find a polymer of one form or another,

135
00:04:50,200 --> 00:04:52,600
 and we humans have gotten so good at making them

136
00:04:52,600 --> 00:04:54,160
 that we can tune their properties

137
00:04:54,160 --> 00:04:56,160
 for very specific problems.

138
00:04:56,160 --> 00:04:58,000
 Cameras do this in a bunch of different ways

139
00:04:58,000 --> 00:04:59,600
 by changing the relative amounts

140
00:04:59,600 --> 00:05:02,640
 of strong cross-links versus weak van der Waals bonds,

141
00:05:02,640 --> 00:05:04,560
 by changing the particular monomers

142
00:05:04,560 --> 00:05:06,000
 that make up a polymer chain,

143
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:09,320
 by sometimes substituting in one in every end element

144
00:05:09,320 --> 00:05:11,000
 of the chain with a different monomer.

145
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:13,240
 By sometimes changing one particular monomer

146
00:05:13,240 --> 00:05:15,440
 to another, by only changing one element

147
00:05:15,440 --> 00:05:17,360
 on one finger of the monomer.

148
00:05:17,360 --> 00:05:19,600
 And by turning these knobs, you can craft polymers

149
00:05:19,600 --> 00:05:21,160
 that are stiff or strong.

150
00:05:21,160 --> 00:05:23,320
 Polymers which have great optical clarity.

151
00:05:23,320 --> 00:05:26,000
 Polymers that are extremely resistant to high temperatures.

152
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:27,800
 Polymers that mimic other materials,

153
00:05:27,800 --> 00:05:29,600
 either in their properties or their appearance.

154
00:05:29,600 --> 00:05:32,240
 And polymers that are super duper recyclable.

155
00:05:32,240 --> 00:05:33,840
 Well, I'm not trying to make a chemistry of you,

156
00:05:33,840 --> 00:05:36,200
 and we won't be talking about how you kind of achieve

157
00:05:36,200 --> 00:05:37,560
 these specific tasks.

158
00:05:37,560 --> 00:05:39,600
 It's important you note that some polymers

159
00:05:39,600 --> 00:05:41,960
 are highly tuned for a particular problem type.

160
00:05:41,960 --> 00:05:44,680
 And unlike some metals which are quite versatile

161
00:05:44,680 --> 00:05:46,960
 and can be used in a range of different applications,

162
00:05:46,960 --> 00:05:48,600
 polymers have generally been made

163
00:05:48,600 --> 00:05:50,920
 with a particular application in mind.

164
00:05:50,920 --> 00:05:53,440
 If a particular polymer isn't well suited to your problem,

165
00:05:53,440 --> 00:05:54,880
 it's likely that there's another one

166
00:05:54,880 --> 00:05:56,560
 which is very suited to your problem.

167
00:05:56,560 --> 00:05:57,840
 You've just got to go and find it.

168
00:05:57,840 --> 00:06:00,200
 To finish up, before we move on to mechanical properties,

169
00:06:00,200 --> 00:06:01,960
 I'm going to talk just a little bit

170
00:06:01,960 --> 00:06:04,240
 about how polymers are made.

171
00:06:04,240 --> 00:06:06,000
 I've already told you that polymers consist

172
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:09,680
 of long repeating chains of small base units called monomers.

173
00:06:09,680 --> 00:06:12,320
 But what I haven't told you is how these monomers

174
00:06:12,320 --> 00:06:15,000
 end up connecting together to make up these polymer chains.

175
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:16,960
 And it's actually super duper interesting.

176
00:06:16,960 --> 00:06:19,280
 Most monomers, the base units of polymers

177
00:06:19,280 --> 00:06:22,960
 are in and of themselves relatively stable compounds.

178
00:06:22,960 --> 00:06:25,920
 Monomers usually don't have any free electrons kicking around.

179
00:06:25,920 --> 00:06:27,480
 They have full valence shells.

180
00:06:27,480 --> 00:06:28,920
 There are a couple of different ways

181
00:06:28,920 --> 00:06:31,080
 that you can force a monomer to begin joining

182
00:06:31,080 --> 00:06:33,120
 with other monomers to make a polymer.

183
00:06:33,120 --> 00:06:36,000
 One of them is called addition polymerization.

184
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:38,000
 In this regime, monomers which have a double

185
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:40,640
 or triple covalent bond at their backbone are used.

186
00:06:40,640 --> 00:06:43,200
 These double and triple bonds aren't particularly stable.

187
00:06:43,200 --> 00:06:45,520
 And so when provided sufficient motivation

188
00:06:45,520 --> 00:06:48,920
 in the form of heat or some sort of catalyzing initiator,

189
00:06:48,920 --> 00:06:51,760
 they break apart, leaving a free electron side

190
00:06:51,760 --> 00:06:53,120
 at the end of the monomer.

191
00:06:53,120 --> 00:06:54,960
 Then this monomer meets another monomer

192
00:06:54,960 --> 00:06:57,520
 who's also been broken apart and the two of them,

193
00:06:57,520 --> 00:07:01,080
 each having a free electron to contribute covalently bond,

194
00:07:01,080 --> 00:07:02,240
 joining together.

195
00:07:02,240 --> 00:07:04,920
 This process repeats over and over again

196
00:07:04,920 --> 00:07:07,120
 as you grow and grow and grow the chain

197
00:07:07,120 --> 00:07:09,280
 until finally either two chains meet up

198
00:07:09,280 --> 00:07:11,120
 and the reaction is terminated.

199
00:07:11,120 --> 00:07:12,800
 Or in the same way that a catalyst

200
00:07:12,800 --> 00:07:14,600
 was put into and initiate the reaction

201
00:07:14,600 --> 00:07:17,040
 and now the chemical is added to terminate it.

202
00:07:17,040 --> 00:07:19,880
 These days, the monomers that we use to make our polymers

203
00:07:19,880 --> 00:07:22,200
 are largely derived from fossil fuels.

204
00:07:22,200 --> 00:07:24,560
 For example, in large refineries crude oil

205
00:07:24,560 --> 00:07:27,720
 and natural gas are converted into ethane and propane

206
00:07:27,720 --> 00:07:30,320
 before being sent to what are called cracky plants

207
00:07:30,320 --> 00:07:34,040
 to convert these into the monomers ethylene and propylene.

208
00:07:34,040 --> 00:07:35,400
 These monomers are then packaged up

209
00:07:35,400 --> 00:07:37,080
 and shipped and transported to places

210
00:07:37,080 --> 00:07:38,600
 that will polymerize them

211
00:07:38,600 --> 00:07:41,160
 either through additional condensation polymerization

212
00:07:41,160 --> 00:07:44,000
 to make the polymers that we know and love.

213
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:46,160
 When where this polymerization process happens

214
00:07:46,160 --> 00:07:48,360
 is largely a function of the specific polymer

215
00:07:48,360 --> 00:07:49,320
 that you're talking about.

216
00:07:49,320 --> 00:07:51,120
 Some polymers are made in bulk quantities

217
00:07:51,120 --> 00:07:52,840
 turned into little plastic pellets

218
00:07:52,840 --> 00:07:54,560
 that are shipped around the world and later

219
00:07:54,560 --> 00:07:57,600
 are melted down in cast or rejected mold into parts.

220
00:07:57,600 --> 00:07:59,320
 Some polymers can't be remelted

221
00:07:59,320 --> 00:08:01,280
 and as a result need to be polymerized

222
00:08:01,280 --> 00:08:04,000
 shortly before being turned into their final shape.

223
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:04,800
 We'll talk a lot more

224
00:08:04,800 --> 00:08:07,720
 about manufacturing and supply of polymers next week.
